WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.362
In this film you get the data out of a FundsXML file: you query it, you transform it, and you read a very large file as a stream, with real code.

2
00:00:09.412 --> 00:00:11.083
It takes about five minutes.

3
00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:15.636
Every value in a FundsXML file has a clear path.

4
00:00:15.686 --> 00:00:23.109
FundsXML has no XML namespace, so the paths are short: FundsXML4, Funds, Fund, and so on down to the value.

5
00:00:23.159 --> 00:00:26.951
Positions and assets are joined through their UniqueID.

6
00:00:27.001 --> 00:00:36.101
With XPath you select values, with XQuery you build new results from them, and with XSLT you turn a whole file into another format.

7
00:00:37.066 --> 00:00:39.162
Let us ask the file a question.

8
00:00:39.212 --> 00:00:41.793
Press Control K for the Query Console.

9
00:00:41.843 --> 00:00:46.708
In the Snippets menu, the FundsXML extension offers ready-made queries.

10
00:00:46.758 --> 00:00:51.000
Choose Top 10 positions, load it into the console, and run it.

11
00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:59.612
Click Table to see the result as a list: the ten largest positions, each with the name from the master data, its value and its weight.

12
00:00:59.662 --> 00:01:05.531
The query is written in XQuery, so you can open it, adapt it, and save it as a snippet of your own.

13
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:17.329
More ready-made queries come with the extension and with the public examples: values aggregated by asset type, a summary of each fund, and a look-through that expands holdings of other funds.

14
00:01:17.379 --> 00:01:20.746
Use them as a starting point for your own reports and checks.

15
00:01:21.996 --> 00:01:23.393
Now transform the file.

16
00:01:23.443 --> 00:01:31.503
In the Transform panel, the stylesheet positions to CSV from the public examples is selected, and the FundsXML file is the input.

17
00:01:31.553 --> 00:01:32.132
Click Run.

18
00:01:32.182 --> 00:01:41.563
The result is a CSV file with one row per position: the fund, the instrument, its identifiers, quantity, price and value, joined with the master data.

19
00:01:41.613 --> 00:01:43.953
Ready for a spreadsheet or a database.

20
00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:52.755
The stylesheet picks the one instrument block of each position generically, so the same columns work for shares, bonds and fund units alike.

21
00:01:52.805 --> 00:01:59.168
The same stylesheet runs on the command line with Saxon, so the export can be part of a scheduled job.

22
00:02:00.318 --> 00:02:01.746
What about large files?

23
00:02:01.796 --> 00:02:07.591
A full delivery with many funds and thousands of positions can reach hundreds of megabytes.

24
00:02:07.641 --> 00:02:11.574
Loading it all into memory is simple, and fine for most files.

25
00:02:11.624 --> 00:02:14.207
For very large ones, read it as a stream.

26
00:02:14.257 --> 00:02:19.924
Here is the streaming example from the FundsXML examples repository, in Python with lxml.

27
00:02:19.974 --> 00:02:22.751
Iterparse hands over one position at a time.

28
00:02:22.801 --> 00:02:31.743
The loop adds up value and weight, and then frees the element and everything before it, so the memory stays flat, however large the file is.

29
00:02:33.093 --> 00:02:34.821
Let us try it on a large file.

30
00:02:34.871 --> 00:02:43.888
The examples repository also has a script that writes a sample with any number of positions: here half a million, a file of a hundred and five megabytes.

31
00:02:43.938 --> 00:02:49.458
The streaming script reads it in about five seconds, with a peak memory of eighteen megabytes.

32
00:02:49.508 --> 00:02:52.658
And the weights add up to exactly one hundred percent.

33
00:02:54.008 --> 00:02:56.390
Many teams prefer to work with classes.

34
00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:03.372
Generate them from the schema: JAXB for Java, xsdata for Python, or the XML schema tools of dot net for C sharp.

35
00:03:03.422 --> 00:03:09.419
The generated code reads and writes valid documents, and the compiler helps you with every field.

36
00:03:09.469 --> 00:03:16.650
Because the schema is large, generate only once per version, and keep the classes apart from your own business code.

37
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.072
And how does the data get into a database, every day, reliably?

38
00:03:22.122 --> 00:03:25.801
That is the subject of the series FundsXML in production.

39
00:03:25.851 --> 00:03:35.025
It builds on a complete example you can download: a PostgreSQL database, a loader for FundsXML files, and a generator that writes them again.

40
00:03:36.375 --> 00:03:38.344
That brings the series together.

41
00:03:38.394 --> 00:03:50.266
You set up the tools, took a delivery apart, read funds, share classes and portfolios, saw transactions and regulatory templates, validated a file in two stages, and got the data out again.

42
00:03:50.316 --> 00:03:56.511
The schema, the examples and the documentation are free, so you can start with your own data today.

43
00:03:57.861 --> 00:04:01.213
That was the last film of the series for developers.

44
00:04:01.263 --> 00:04:07.887
If you want to see how FundsXML runs in real systems, continue with the series FundsXML in production.

45
00:04:07.937 --> 00:04:12.204
Thanks for watching, and good luck with your own FundsXML project.
